# DESCRIPTION

Radharc owns the screen and shows clients' windows on it.
It runs only in the desktop's images (`vx boot --desktop`, vx-build(1)), as the service `windows`, which each client that uses it reaches at `/svc/windows`.

Radharc maps the framebuffer that the boot data names, as console(4) does, and composes on the CPU into a copy in memory, copying only what changed to the framebuffer.
When it starts it takes the framebuffer from the console through the console's `handoff` (radharc-handoff(5)), and the console draws into a window from then on.

A client opens a window from `compositor` (radharc-compositor(5)), and each window is a channel of its own (radharc-window(5)).
A program usually does this through the SDK (sdk(2)).
A window's pixels are images the client shares: 32-bit `0x00RRGGBB` words, sRGB, in a VMO, each with an acquire counter that the client raises once it has drawn, and a release counter that Radharc raises once it no longer reads the image.
The client presents layers, solid colours or images at places in the window, drawn for the size of a configure Radharc sent it.
An image drawn for an older size is shown at its own size, cropped, never stretched.

An image is opaque sRGB unless the client tags it (`tagImage`): a tag names its pixels' transfer, primaries and alpha.
Radharc composes only sRGB with BT.709's primaries, and refuses a tag naming another transfer or other primaries with the error that names it (`transfer`, `primaries`).
An image tagged with alpha, premultiplied or straight, has it in each pixel's top byte, and is blended over the layers beneath it in linear light (light(2)); an opaque image, a solid layer and the decorations are copied.

Radharc composes at a vblank of its own, 60 a second, which nothing drives: the boot framebuffer has none.
A change, such as a present or the pointer moving, wakes a thread that waits for the next vblank, takes each window's oldest present whose images are drawn, composes once, and sends each of those windows a `frame` giving back the present's credit.
A window may have 1 to 3 presents queued, its latency; one more is refused as `noCredit`.
A window that is hidden, or covered by one above it, has its presents taken about once a second.
With nothing changing, the thread sleeps.
The same thread keeps two timers: the desktop goes idle 10 seconds after the last key or pointer event, and a window showing the clock has it changed at each minute.
It runs under a deadline profile of 4 ms in every 16.7 ms when the manifest gives Radharc the system resource.

Windows are managed as Be's were.
A press selects a window at once, and it rises on release unless the press became a drag.
Dragging its tab moves it, its corner grip resizes it, and a moved window snaps to the screen's edges within 12 pixels.
The tab's close box asks the client to close the window, and its zoom box fills the screen less the decorations, or goes back.
A new window opens cascaded from the last and is focused.
The desk and the cursor are drawn in the `desktop` part and the `desk` and `ink` roles of the theme in force (theme(6)).
A window's frame, its tab with its title and Be's close and zoom boxes and its body, is drawn by UICore's `Chrome` (uicore(2)) in the `window.tab`, `window.tab.widget` and `window.body` parts and their `.focused` states, with their materials, corners and glow, and the title in the face the theme's `ui` typeface names, chosen from the faces in `/lib/fonts`; the tab's and the body's sizes, and where the boxes are, are the theme's too.
A frame is drawn once for a window's title and focus, small, on a thread of Radharc's own, and made at the window's size from that in nine slices: its corners and tab as drawn, its edges their middle repeated; until it is drawn, the window keeps the frame it had, or a plain one of the theme's colours.
Its edges are blended over what is beneath them, so the room beside the tab, round or cut corners and a glow show the windows under them.
Without faces in `/lib/fonts`, every frame is the plain one.

Radharc serves the theme.
It reads the installed themes from `/lib/theme` when it starts, and puts Platinum Indigo in force, or the base theme if Platinum Indigo isn't installed or fails its check.
Every client's tree has the same theme tree at `theme`, and a change made through one is everyone's: `ctl` loads another theme, chooses a variant, or sets a role over the theme, as the Appearance editor does.
Each change resolves the whole stack again (theme(6)) and checks it as `theme check` does; a theme that doesn't resolve or fails the check is refused with the reason, and the theme before it stays.
A change redraws the screen, raises the theme's generation, writes a line to each reader of `events`, and makes `resolved` the new theme.
`resolved` is memory, read only, holding one message in the wire format: radharc-themefile(5)'s `resolved` event, which an app maps and reads its theme from without parsing a theme file.
A client that mapped it keeps the theme it mapped; the file is the new one's after a change, so an app maps it again when `events` tells it to.

Radharc reads the keyboard from kbdfs(4)'s `/dev/kbd` and the pointer from mouse(4)'s `/dev/mouse`.
Keys go to the focused window, with the HID usage of the key, its unshifted character and the modifiers held after it; the usage is the keymap's for the rune `/dev/kbd` names.
The pointer moves the cursor and works the windows, and the window under it hears where it is in its own content, its buttons (1 the left, 2 the right, 3 the middle) and the wheel's notches.
A window's input comes on a ring of its own, 256 events of 40 bytes, with a counter as its doorbell; a window that does not read its ring loses what does not fit.

Radharc draws the trusted prompt, which only the user's own keyboard and pointer answer.
A client whose manifest says `grant windows prompt` (factotum's does) asks a question by opening `prompt`, writing it, and reading the answer, which the read waits for; closing the file before then withdraws the question.
Questions wait in turn, the oldest on screen.
The prompt is a panel over every window, the rest of the screen dimmed to a quarter of its light, drawn in colours of its own that no theme reaches: a black strip, a seal, and its buttons.
It names the app it asks for, as the question gives it, and the client that asked, as the launcher names it: `mail, through factotum`.
While it is up, no window gets a key, a click or the pointer; the pointer moves only the cursor.
A click lands on a button only if the button went down and came up on it, half a second at least after the question showed, so that a click aimed at what was there before doesn't answer it; a key that answers waits the same half second.
Escape is deny.
A key's question has fields to type, a secret's drawn as bullets; tab goes to the next field, and return answers once.
Its text is what `/dev/kbd` says was typed, shifted as typed.
The prompt also frames the file chooser when a window calls `choose` (radharc-window(5)): a client whose manifest says `grant windows chooser` (tracker(4)) registers a `Chooser` channel at `chooser` (radharc-chooserprovider(5), radharc-chooser(5)), Radharc opens a view for it inside the prompt's panel and forwards keys, pointer and wheel to the view while the prompt's Cancel and Open or Save buttons stay under the half-second rule, and on confirm passes the confined file handle from `Chooser.confirm` back to the calling window (`chosen`).
`pixel` reads the panel as black, so that what a client reads from the screen never holds it.

Radharc holds the clipboard: one entry, the representations of one thing, each a MIME type and its bytes, such as a text and an image of the same selection.
Radharc keeps a copy of the entry, so it lasts after the client that wrote it has gone, until another entry replaces it whole or Radharc stops.
Any client writes it: `snarf` takes text, as rio's does, and `clip/new` a whole entry; either is committed when the file is closed.
Only the client whose window is focused reads it, and only once that window has had a key or a press since it was focused, which a paste always has; a client in the background, or one watching the clipboard, is refused until then.
An entry marked secret, as a password field's copy is, is cleared 30 seconds after it was committed.
A client whose manifest says `grant windows clipboard` (notices(4), which keeps the clipboard's history) reads each entry committed after it opened `clipboard`, but for secret ones, with who wrote it.

Radharc draws the clock in a window that asks for it with `showClock` (radharc-window(5)), as deskbar(4) does: the time, UTC, `HH:MM` or `h:MM AM`, right-aligned and centred in an area of the window's content, over what the window drew there, in the theme's `deskbar.clock` part.
The face for each time is drawn on the frames' thread, the next minute's ahead of it, and swapped in at the minute by a frame that composes only the clock's area, so the window's program never wakes for the time; until a face is drawn, or without faces in `/lib/fonts`, the time is in the console's 8 by 16 font in the tab's ink.

Radharc has workspaces, 4 unless Settings' `Workspaces:Count` says otherwise (1 to 9), and shows one at a time.
A window opens on the workspace shown, and is on that one only; the desktop, panels and views are on every workspace, and a popup is where its owner is.
Showing another workspace hides the windows of the one shown and shows its own, and focus goes to the window last focused there; raising a window on another workspace (`top`, `show`) shows that workspace.
Each workspace is `space` or `tile`.
In `space` a window is where it was put: stacking, with the edges snapping.
In `tile` a layout places its windows: Radharc asks the workspace's layout, by name (master-stack(4) is the first), for a frame for each window in the workspace's order, and puts them there; a new window comes first in the order, and the zoom box makes a window first.
A tiled window doesn't move or resize by its tab or grip, and `move` and `resize` change only the frame it will have in `space`.
Fullscreen windows, hidden ones, the desktop, panels, views and popups are not tiled.
Both arrangements are kept: a window keeps its `space` frame while it is tiled, and going back to `space` puts every window where it was, nothing worked out from the tiles.
A window sent to another workspace takes that workspace's mode.

A layout is a process of its own, holding no state: a manifest that says `grant windows layout` reaches `layout` (radharc-layoutprovider(5)), and registers a channel on which it answers radharc-layout(5).
Radharc keeps each workspace's order and the layout's parameters, and passes them in each call, so a layout restarted or replaced, by registering under the name another holds, loses nothing.
Its calls are made on a thread of Radharc's own, never under its lock, each with a deadline of 50 ms; a layout that fails or is late is dropped, and its workspaces keep the frames they have until one registers under its name again.
A layout sees window ids and sizes only, never titles or pixels.
`ctl`'s `act` passes a verb to the shown workspace's layout, and the order, focus and parameters it answers are kept.

Radharc keeps in Settings (settings(2)), when `/data` has a volume: each workspace's mode, layout and parameters in `Workspaces` (`Workspaces:<n>:Mode`, `:Layout`, `:Params`), read when it starts; and in `Frames`, for each app, the `space` frame of its last window closed (`Frames:<app>` is `<workspace> <x> <y> <width> <height>`), where the app's next window opens if it is the app's only one and hasn't placed itself.
An app whose windows are documents keeps their frames itself, as tracker(4) does.

Radharc serves `shortcuts` (radharc-shortcuts(5)) to a client whose manifest says `grant windows shortcuts`, once the user allows it.
A chord registered there is matched before the focused window hears a key, and taken with its key-up: the focused window never sees it.
A recorded answer in Settings' `Grants` (`Grants:<app>.shortcuts`) serves or refuses it at once; with none, or none given this session, Radharc asks on the trusted prompt itself, holding the channel meanwhile: `always` serves it and records the grant, `This session` serves it until Radharc restarts, and deny closes it.
A recorded grant revoked, as Grants in preferences(4) or `attr -d` does, closes the channels it let in.
chords(4) holds the desktop's chords.

Radharc locks the screen on `ctl`'s `lock`, on Control-Alt-Delete, and after `Lock:Minutes` minutes without input, from Settings' `Lock` area (10 if unset, 0 never, followed as it changes).
Locked, the screen is black but for the unlock question, drawn as the prompt is, with one button, Unlock; every key and button goes to it or nowhere, chords aren't taken, no window hears anything, so none may read the clipboard, and questions other than the unlock question wait until the screen is unlocked.
Escape empties what was typed.
A client whose manifest says `grant windows lock` (factotum(4)) unlocks it: it writes `ready` to `lock` when it can, asks the unlock question on `prompt`, checks the password itself, and writes `unlock`.
With no ready client the screen can't be locked, so it is never locked with no way back.
Control-Alt-Delete is Radharc's alone, whatever the screen shows: unlocked, it locks, and locked, it empties the password typed.
A window can draw a copy of the lock screen, but never sees this chord, so pressing it before typing a password always reaches the real one.
Radharc keeps `Lock:Locked` in Settings as it locks and unlocks, and starts locked if it is set.

A client may also open a view, a window shown only inside another window, by a token: a channel end, which the window that shows it passes to `embed`.
A window may be fullscreen, place itself, be the desktop (beneath every other window, undecorated), a panel (above every other window) or a popup of another window; the window list, `windows` (radharc-windows(5)), lists them and moves, sizes, raises, hides and closes them.

The manifest:

```text
service windows
program radharc
resource mmio
resource system
bootdata
export per-client
use console
mount /dev kbdfs
mount /dev mouse after
mount /lib/theme bootfs/lib/theme
mount /lib/fonts bootfs/lib/fonts
mount /data fs/volume optional
restart on-failure
```

# FILES

Each client has its own tree at `/svc/windows`; the files are the same in each.

: `status`
  Read only.
  A line each: the screen (`screen <w>x<h>`); the framebuffer (`display framebuffer <w>x<h> stride <s> <format>`); the input read (`input keys /dev/kbd, pointer /dev/mouse`, or `no keys`, `no pointer`); the vblank (`vblank synthetic <ns> ns`); the latch thread's profile (`latch deadline 4 ms of each 16.7 ms, admitted`, `refused (<reason>)` or `none (...)`); the frames latched (`latches <n>`); how late the latch woke and how long after its vblank the frame was out, over the last 120 frames (`late median <n> us max <n> us`, `shown ...`); the windows (`windows <n>`), the focused one (`focused <id>`), the frames composed (`frames <n>`), the pointer (`pointer <x> <y>`), the input routed (`keys <n> pointer events <n>`), and the prompt's questions queued, those asked since it started, and the clicks and keys that came too soon (`prompts <n> asked <n> ignored <n>`); the clipboard's entries committed since it started, the reads it refused, and the entries given to `clipboard` (`clip commits <n> refused <n> fed <n>`); the drags started and those dropped (`drags <n> drops <n>`); the windows showing the clock and the frames that changed only the clocks (`clocks <n> frames <n>`); `idle` or `active`; the workspace shown (`workspace <n> of <count>`); the layouts registered, and the verbs they refused and the calls that failed or were late (`layouts <name>... refused <n> failed <n>`, `-` for none); the frames the last mode switch of the shown workspace took to be on screen, and the switches timed (`switch <n> frames switches <n>`); the `shortcuts` holders, the chords they hold, and the chords pressed (`shortcuts <n> chords <n> pressed <n>`); and the lock, the clients ready to unlock, the locks, the unlocks and the Control-Alt-Deletes (`lock locked|unlocked ready <n> locks <n> unlocks <n> attentions <n>`).
: `list`
  Read only.
  A line a window, bottom to top: its id, its content's x, y, width and height on the screen, `focused` or `-`, and its title.
  A view, shown only inside a window, has no line.
: `workspaces`
  Read only.
  A line a workspace: its number, `space` or `tile`, its layout, `current` or `-`, its windows, and its layout's parameters if it has any.
: `ctl`
  A write is one command: `lock` locks the screen, failing if nothing could unlock it; `workspace <n>` shows workspace `n`; `send <n> [<id>]` moves a window (the focused one if none is named) to workspace `n`; `mode tile|space|toggle [<n>]` sets a workspace's mode (the shown one's if none is named); `layout <name> [<n>]` chooses its layout; `act <verb>...` passes a verb to the shown workspace's layout.
  A command not understood, or a workspace or window that isn't there, fails with `invalid`; `act` fails only if the shown workspace isn't tiled.
  The same commands are radharc-windows(5)'s `workspace`, `send`, `setMode` and `act`.
: `layout`
  Only with `grant windows layout`: radharc-layoutprovider(5), where a layout registers.
: `shortcuts`
  Only with `grant windows shortcuts`, served once the user allows it: radharc-shortcuts(5).
: `idle`
  Read only: `idle` or `active`, whether 10 seconds have passed since the last key or pointer event.
  Each channel opened at it reads the line at once, and then each read waits until it changes, so a reader wakes only when someone comes to the desktop or leaves it.
: `pacing`
  Read only: `p99 <n>`, the 99th percentile of how late the latch woke, in microseconds, over the last 120 frames.
  Read as `idle` is, when it has moved 100 microseconds; with nothing composing it doesn't change.
: `pixel`
  A write of `x y` chooses a pixel of the screen; a read gives the composed pixel there as six hex digits, `rrggbb`, or `outside`, and `000000` within the trusted prompt.
: `prompt`
  Only with `grant windows prompt`.
  Each open asks one question: a write of one ndb(6) entry, a read that waits for its answer, and gives it once.
  The entry's `ask=confirm` asks to allow something, and `ask=key` asks for a key's fields: each attribute written `name?` is a field, `!name?` a secret one.
  `ask=unlock`, with a field, is the lock screen's question, asked only by a client granted `lock`.
  `app=` names the app it asks for (the asker if none); `answers=` the answers it offers besides deny, from `once`, `session` and `always` (`once` if none); its other attributes say what it asks about, shown as written, 256 bytes at most, and no secret.
  The answer is a line: the answer's word, and for a key its fields after it, `once user=jk !password=secret`.
  A write that isn't such an entry, or a second write, fails with `invalid`.
: `snarf`
  The clipboard's text.
  A read gives the entry's `text/plain` representation, or nothing if it has none; it fails with `denied` unless this client's window is focused and has had a key or a press since.
  Writes are collected, 1 MiB at most, and replace the clipboard with an entry of that text, `text/plain;charset=utf-8`, when the file is closed; closed without a write, it changes nothing.
: `clip/`
  The entry's representations, a file each, named by its type without parameters, lower case, with `/` written as `.` (`text.plain`, `image.png`), read as `snarf` is; with `new`.
  Listed, or walked into, by a client that may not read the clipboard, it holds only `new`.
: `clip/new`
  Write only: a whole entry, on one open.
  Each representation is a line, `type=<mime type> length=<n>`, and then its `n` bytes; the first line may add `secret=1`, an entry cleared 30 seconds after it is committed.
  An entry has 1 to 16 representations, no two of one name, 16 MiB in all at most, and `text/plain` 1 MiB at most.
  It replaces the clipboard when the file is closed, if what was written ends where a representation does; a write that breaks these rules fails with `invalid`, and the entry is not committed.
: `drag`
  Write only: an entry, as `clip/new` takes one, which starts a drag when the file is closed, if the left button pressed on the content of one of this client's windows is still held; the clipboard is untouched.
  Radharc draws a badge beside the cursor, filled in the focused tab's colour over a window that would take the drag and in the window body's elsewhere.
  The window under the pointer hears `dragOver` with the entry's types and where the pointer is, again as it moves, and `dragLeft` when it leaves; it answers with `acceptDrag` and the types it would take (radharc-window(5)).
  Released over a window that accepted some, the drag drops: that window hears `drop` with the types it accepted, and the window it came from `dragEnded`, dropped; released anywhere else, or cancelled with Escape, it ends with no drop.
: `drop/`
  What was dropped on one of this client's windows, a file a representation, named as `clip`'s, until that window's next key or press; empty otherwise.
: `lock`
  Only with `grant windows lock`: a read gives `locked` or `unlocked`, and each later read waits until that changes; a write of `ready` (optionally `ready user=<name>`) says the client can unlock, while its file is open, and then `unlock` unlocks.
: `clipboard`
  Only with `grant windows clipboard`: read only, each entry committed after the file was opened that isn't secret, in order, a read waiting for the next.
  Each is a line, `entry source=<app> origin=<title> count=<n>`, then its `n` representations as `clip/new` takes them; the app is the writer's window's, or the client's name, and the title its focused window's, or its first's.
  The last 8 entries are kept for a reader, so one that falls further behind misses the older ones.
: `compositor`
  A service: radharc-compositor(5).
: `chooser`
  Only with `grant windows chooser`: radharc-chooserprovider(5), where tracker(4) registers the file chooser (radharc-chooser(5)).
: `windows`
  A service: radharc-windows(5), the window list, its changes and its verbs.
: `theme/active`
  Read only.
  The theme in force: `theme <name>`, `variant <name>` or `variant none`, `generation <n>`, a line each, then a line `set <role> <colour>` for each role set over it.
: `theme/themes/`
  The installed themes, a file each, as `/lib/theme` holds them; the base is built in and isn't here.
: `theme/ctl`
  A write is one command: `load <name>` puts a theme in force, without a variant or roles set; `variant <name>` chooses a variant of it, and `variant none` none; `set <role> <colour>` sets a role over it until the next `load` (a colour as theme(6) writes one, or another role).
  A write refused fails with `invalid`, and a read gives the reason, as `theme check` words it, or nothing if the last command passed.
: `theme/events`
  Read only, and a read waits.
  Each reader gets its own lines from when it opened the file: `theme <name> variant <name> generation <n>` after each change, and `refused <reason>` for a command refused.
: `theme/resolved`
  Read only, memory: the theme in force, resolved, as one message in the wire format (radharc-themefile(5)).

